[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Typography in Tailwind CSS: Font Families, Leading, and Tracking"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Typography is the backbone of readable and aesthetically pleasing web design. Tailwind CSS provides a robust, highly customizable system for styling text out of the box. In this tutorial, you will learn how to configure and apply font families, leading (line height), and tracking (letter spacing) to your web applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Setting Font Families"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tailwind comes with three default font families: sans, serif, and mono. You can apply these directly using utility classes like font-sans, font-serif, or font-mono."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<p class="font-sans">This is sans-serif text.</p>
<p class="font-serif">This is serif text.</p>
<p class="font-mono">This is monospace text.</p>"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To add custom font families, you can extend the theme configuration in your tailwind.config.js file."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"module.exports = {
  theme: {
    extend: {
      fontFamily: {
        custom: ['"Inter"', 'sans-serif'],
      },
    },
  },
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Controlling Leading (Line Height)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Leading refers to the vertical space between lines of text. Tailwind provides utility classes ranging from tight leading to loose leading, making it easy to control readability."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"leading-none: Sets line height to 1."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"leading-tight: Sets line height to 1.25."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"leading-normal: Sets line height to 1.5."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"leading-loose: Sets line height to 2."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
  This paragraph uses a relaxed line height, improving readability for longer blocks of descriptive content.
</p>"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Adjusting Tracking (Letter Spacing)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tracking, or letter spacing, allows you to increase or decrease the space between characters. This is especially useful for styling uppercase headings or modern minimalist UI components."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"tracking-tighter: -0.05em"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"tracking-normal: 0em"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"tracking-widest: 0.1em"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"<h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-widest uppercase">
  Section Heading
</h2>"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining custom font families, precise leading, and balanced tracking, you can drastically elevate the visual hierarchy and professional feel of your Tailwind CSS projects."}]
